verdict.reasoning:
I tried to refute this and could not; the finding is real.

1. Archive alerts do reach the check. The window's watch (window.py:663-698) calls check_now -> selfcheck.run_checks. run_checks fails on any alert with severity 3 in status.json (selfcheck.py:117-119). The recorder adds the archive copier's alerts to its own status.json alerts (service.py:755-768, through RecorderArchiveView.evaluate at archive/status.py:190-222). A-004 and A-006 are worked out from the ledger and are CRITICAL (status.py:194-197), so they stay open across a recorder restart. A-007 turns CRITICAL after 30 minutes. A copied A-002 "drive full" is also CRITICAL. None of these has anything to do with the settings change being watched.

2. The rollback cannot be avoided. With such an alert open, passes never reaches 3. After 180 s, _watch_tick calls rollback_to_known_good whenever <config>.good exists and differs from the current file (window.py:684-694). The first watch_after_change check (window.py:653) only requires an administrator, and saving settings already requires one.

3. <config>.good can be stale. mark_known_good is only called from window.check_now (window.py:643), on a pass while unlocked as administrator. That happens only on "Check everything" (status_pages.py:712) or on watch passes. Nothing refreshes it in the background. launcher/setcamera.py never touches .good, and the documented camera-later procedure (07_SECURITY.md:303) is exactly 'Sentinel.exe set-camera' on the command line. While A-004/A-006 stay open, no check can pass, so .good also cannot be refreshed.

4. I reproduced it with a script outside the repo using the real selfcheck/setup code:
   - set up with camera later, check passes, mark_known_good;
   - add the camera by rewriting the config (pending=False);
   - a healthy recording status plus an A-004 CRITICAL alert gives run_checks ok=False ("No critical alerts: A-004");
   - same_settings(good, current) is False;
   - restore_known_good puts camera.pending back to True.

   So the rollback restores the pending-camera config and the recorder stops recording. The second watch started by rollback_to_known_good also fails (A-004 is still open). It then shows "There are no earlier working settings to go back to". That is misleading too, because the message blames the settings.

Severity: in the common case, any save made while an archive alert is open is silently reverted to the last checked settings and the recorder is restarted. The alert is easy to hit at a remote site with an overdue swap or an unacknowledged A-006. That is wrong behaviour with a misleading "did not work with the new settings" message. In the stale-.good case it stops recording until a technician comes. That needs a fairly specific but documented order of events (camera-later, then CLI set-camera, then no admin Check everything afterwards), so I rate it high rather than critical.

Fix: base the watch's verdict only on what a settings change can break: the settings load, the recorder is running with a fresh status, the state is healthy, and the camera link is checked only if the camera section changed. Ignore alert codes that were already open when the save was made, and archive A-0xx alerts in general. Roll back to the backup the save itself made, not to a .good that may be older than the current file. Also refresh .good when set-camera succeeds.

coverage.covered:
I read all of src/sentinel/export (engine, ledger, planner, layout, fileio, manifest, media, contentcheck, target, status, __main__, __init__) and src/sentinel/storage/monitor.py, along with docs/04 §6, the export items in docs/REVIEW-2026-11-10.md (M3, M4 and L10 were checked and their fixes are in place), and the parts of other code my area depends on: retention/manager.py, the finalizer's sealing and timestamps, the service janitor, alerts E-001..E-007, the db export DDL, clock formats, and the GUI Export page.

I confirmed findings 1-4 with throwaway scripts in the scratchpad that use real FFmpeg-made TS segments in a temporary ledger and folder drives: the stuck-segment cap, E-006 for a missing or altered original, the E-007 loop after a crash just after the rename, and the lost verify-content result.

These checks found no defect:
- the sampled picture check on intact segments and on segments truncated mid-packet;
- timestamp format and comparisons;
- DST naming and hour grouping;
- interval and late-segment completeness, apart from the stuck cap;
- budget cut at drive_too_small;
- the no-replace rename and the Windows FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING read-back;
- MP4 written-hash versus uncached read-back;
- the archive-drive refusal.

storage/monitor.py is simple and nothing wrong was found. Not exercised: the Linux O_DIRECT path (on ntfs-3g/FUSE a read error EINVAL would fail the export as E-005, unverified), real HEVC camera footage, and behaviour on a real USB drive. I ran only focused scratch scripts, not the test suite, and made no changes in the repository.
